diff --git a/docs/p6r-sidebar-performance.md b/docs/p6r-sidebar-performance.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..7e061d1607b067e8ca61184cba2be5e5925b174a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/p6r-sidebar-performance.md
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# Compact sidebar trial
+
+This downstream trial is off by default. In the browser console run
+`localStorage.setItem('bb.p6r.compact-sidebar', '1'); location.reload()`.
+To roll back run `localStorage.removeItem('bb.p6r.compact-sidebar'); location.reload()`.
+The switch is per browser origin. It does not require a deployment or server restart.
+A reload is required so every observer chooses the same request owner.
+
+The existing `/api/v1/sidebar-bootstrap` serializer and client hook remain unchanged.
+The trial uses `/api/v1/p6r/sidebar-bootstrap`, `/api/v1/p6r/sidebar-threads?ids=thr_...`
+(up to 200 IDs), and `/api/v1/p6r/sidebar-projects` (project metadata and sections,
+without thread rows). These are additive internal read routes, not new plugin APIs.
+No thread is excluded beyond the existing non-archived, visible, non-deleted scope.
+
+The wire protocol interns complete participant and environment projections. It
+omits only exact default values and project IDs already supplied by their enclosing
+project. The decoder restores the complete existing domain row before publishing
+to the existing TanStack cache and plugin SDK selectors. Non-default values,
+participant order, differing records for the same identity, exact fallback titles,
+paths, attention/read timestamps, activity counters and children are retained.
+The protocol has an explicit version and validates dictionary references.
+
+A single owner per QueryClient shares reads among observers. Observers remain
+passive on the trial path, so legacy invalidations cannot start or cancel HTTP.
+Known thread changes queue bounded row reads; explicit missing IDs remove rows.
+Project/section notifications refresh only metadata. Environment metadata updates
+refresh affected rows. Event appends without relevant background/turn changes do
+not reload navigation. Reconnect, resume, initial acquisition and explicit refresh
+request a baseline. Hidden pages defer work. Failed reads retain data and retry
+with bounded backoff. One physical read runs at a time; subsequent changes merge
+into one pending operation (large ID sets require bounded batches). Releasing the
+last observer retires publication but allows the physical read to finish.
+Optimistic cache edits made during a read and held metadata patches survive its
+late response. Reconnect remains the recovery boundary for missed notifications.
+
+The same switch lets thread-tab persistence join an existing tabs query when no
+cached revision is available. The legacy synchronization read stays in place
+when the switch is off.
+
+## Behavior and verification limits
+
+Sections, project metadata, pin order, parent relationships and all raw row fields
+remain in the common cache. Core search/filter selectors and plugins such as
+thread-list and inbox-sidebar-nerd receive the existing full shape. Inbox mode,
+unread/attention, grouping and collapse decisions remain with their existing
+consumers. No visibility-scoped fetching or thread archive migration is included.
+
+Codec, SQLite route parity, rollback, multiple observers, cancellation storms,
+row moves/creation/removal, concurrent optimistic edits and hidden-page recovery
+have regression coverage. Browser replay measurements use a saved data snapshot
+and fixed notifications, separately from live measurements. They do not establish
+real-user latency, live event rates or production plugin behavior after deployment.
+Plugin consumer acceptance with a verified person's identity remains a deployment
+check. The trial deliberately has no automatic fallback that would conceal errors.
